00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:22.790 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  my design career and my life and design  really started on the potter's wheel  i'm jonathan atler i am a potter  a designer and author  and lover of glamorous spaces  my expertise in design comes from being  in this business for a long time and  having done it all from being a potter  to making furniture and all the stuff  that surrounds you to designing spaces  from the smallest studio apartment to  the biggest hotels you can imagine and  i'm here to share my years of experience  with you  [Music]  [Applause]  so maybe i'll just  walk you through what i'm doing  so i am going to make a pot  [Music]  and i think it's called throwing because  the first thing you do  is throw the wedge of clay  onto the wheel  and then  the guiding principle  of making pots is control  so when making a pot  i always think  i'm in control of the clay the clay is  not in control of me  so  [Music]  the first thing i'm doing is i'm just  going to center the clay on the wheel  and it always wants to be on center  [Music]  water is my friend but not too much  water  and with potter you kind of need to  make a pot with confidence and speed  and intentionality  so the very first process is to center  the clay  the key  to making a pot is to always have your  arms braced  against your legs so that there's  nowhere for the clay to go and you're in  control  then once the clay is centered  you  make the hole in the clay  [Music]  my main goal in making this pot is to  try not to get clay on my white pants  and  i actually started wearing  white pants because as you can see the  clay that i work with  is actually a very light color it's not  like a red clay and so when it  gets on my clothes it kind of dries and  looks white so i started wearing white  jeans because  they were the only pants i could wear  that i could get dirty  and go from day to evening covered in  clay and people might not notice  if you're working with clay that comes  out of a bag it gets really like  set in its ways and  smacking the clay on the ground like i  did is a great workout and it kind of  seems to shock the clay  into  being more plastic  clay  has a mind of its own  and one of the funny things about clay  is  that even after you finish making a pot  the clay wants to keep spinning  and so  one of the things you'll see me do is as  i try to raise up the walls of the pot  my hands are going  up and i'm kind of squeezing the clay  between my  hand on the inside and the outside  but then i will also  sometimes  do the same motion  but just going in a downward motion  because it helps to untwist the clay and  kind of stop it  from  doing its turn  i'm just gonna make  kind of a very classic and traditional  shape  which is kind of nice and full at the  bottom  it just gets a little bit narrower at  the top  and it's incredibly  relaxing and intuitive  and  inevitable form for a pot  it's funny making a pot is actually the  ultimate expression  of  the apollonian versus the dionysian  because the clay  wants to  explode in chaos and fly off the wheel  and i  need to  be in control  of its impulse towards chaos  it's funny i've had a strange journey  and i would hardly say i'm a carefree  person but i never had a plan at all  i kind of just  made pots  sold pots  and kind of let it all unfold  i'm glad i did it that way  for me  clay was almost like a gateway drug  to  a general interest in design  and to a creative life so  you never know what your interests and  hobbies can lead you to it never crossed  my mind in a million years  that i would grow up and become a potter  and  then a retailer  and a furniture designer and a designer  of  restaurants and houses and hotels  all while still  being a potter first and foremost  so now i've gotten the pot to the  approximate size and shape i want and  i'm going to use a  metal rib  to help define  the shape  and smooth the surface  i will trim away the excess clay at the  bottom  um  no clay on my pants or shoes yet  stay tuned  i should be wearing an apron but i'm not  a voila a pot  i actually need to make this for a  sample so i think i shall  we kind of do these like mugs that have  little like faces on them or these pops  that have faces on them  and so this is going to be like a mug  within that family  i want to just have the  slightest of slight little bell shape so  that  it'll make it a little more appealing to  drink from  and because this is a prototype that's  going to be slipcast  i'm trying to make it approximately  20 bigger than i want the final product  to be  so right now it looks like a giant mug  but the finished mug will probably be  kind of muggy  the mug  maybe i'll just do like kind of a big  kind of  like a flared cylinder  [Music]  i first tried pottery at summer camp  when i was 13  and i kind of thought oh i'll give this  a try and from the split second i  touched clay it was  on and a lifelong love affair with  pottery began and it has been the most  rewarding relationship ever  [Music]  being a potter  was kind of the best  training grounds for being a designer  because  all of the concerns and issues that i  address in all of my design work whether  it is making a pot or making a piece of  furniture or designing a space  are all the same and they can all be  boiled down to the issues in making a  pot it's about proportion  it's about silhouette it's about harmony  it's about balance it's about  finishing something well and not doing  it just halfway  the end result of a pot involves color  and pattern and something as simple as a  mug or vase can involve a bazillion  decisions  that are all pretty much analogous to  the decisions you have to make  when  designing a room or house so everything  i needed to know  i learned on this silly potter's wheel  [Music]  with pottery i discovered something that  i loved  and i kind of just leaned into it and  miraculously it led to a fantastic  creative career  [Music]  trying not to get one speck  of clay on my  self  i think i've got this  even without a little  splatter lit  [Music]  because normally i would have like a  towel over my leg and an apron and  all different levels of protection  [Music]  hey voila  a pot  is done

